functions.detail:
Which of the two comes back is detected from the file's bytes, never from its extension, so a mislabelled picture is still a picture. The two arms are a sealed interface, so a `switch` over them needs no `default`:

```java
switch (Files.readFile("logo.png")) {
    case Files.TextFile text -> Views.openText("logo", text.contents());
    case Files.ImageFile picture -> Views.openText("logo", picture.label());
}
```

A relative path resolves against the workspace; an absolute one is read as given, so anything else in this container is readable. Reading an image describes it — label, media type, byte size — and shows nothing: the pixels reach neither the program nor the context window.

Returns: the file's text, or the picture's description

Throws `ToolError`: `ToolErrorCode.NOT_FOUND` for a missing path.

`readFile(String path, int offset, int limit)` — Read a window of lines from a file rather than the whole of it.

The window is honoured only under a capped read policy; where it is not, the whole file comes back and `totalLines` says how much of it the read returned.

Returns: the window of the file's text, or the picture's description

types.detail:
A failure is thrown rather than returned because Java is an exception language: the common path reads as a straight line, and only the calls expected to fail are wrapped. It is unchecked for the same reason — a checked exception would put a `try` around every line of a composed program.

```java
try {
    Views.openText("notes", Files.readTextFile("NOTES.md"));
} catch (ToolError failure) {
    if (failure.code() != ToolErrorCode.NOT_FOUND) {
        throw failure;
    }
    Views.openText("notes", "there are no notes yet");
}
```

An unexpected one is best left to escape: gg reports which call failed, with the stack, and the turn is recoverable.

It is exactly one of the two, and that is the whole reason it is a type rather than two optional arguments: gg can carry out neither "both" nor "neither", and this makes both of them a compile error rather than a refusal at run time.

```java
Delegation.spawnSubagent("reviewer", Delegation.Brief.issue("AUTH-3"));
Delegation.spawnSubagent("worker", Delegation.Brief.prompt("port the fixtures"));
```
